{
  "requirement": "Requirement and reference names MUST have nonempty dot-separated segments containing only ASCII letters, digits, underscores, or hyphens. A `{req}` directive whose full name is invalid, including one whose chapter path gives an invalid namespace, and a role with an invalid name MUST remain literal and MUST NOT define or reference a requirement. For nested chapter paths, a leading `reqs/` and a final `index` or `README` segment MUST be omitted from the requirement namespace.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The reviewed tests adequately cover `requirements.ci.identifiers`.\n\n**Name syntax (nonempty dot-separated segments of ASCII letters, digits, `_` or `-`)**\n- `src/lib.rs#mod[tests]/fn[invalid_names_remain_literal]` asserts `valid_name` is false for these names:\n  - the empty name\n  - `a..b`, `.a` and `a.`\n  - a name with a space\n  - non-ASCII (`café`)\n  - names containing `/`, `&` or a backtick\n- The same test asserts `valid_name` is true for `a`, `a_b.c-d`, `A1.b2` and `x.-_.y`.\n\n**An invalid `{req}` directive stays literal and defines nothing**\n- `invalid_names_remain_literal` asserts that `replace_definitions` leaves `:::{req} bad name` unchanged. The valid sibling directive still renders a badge.\n- The same test asserts that `definition_blocks` returns nothing for invalid `req`, `example` and `definition` openers.\n- `examples_are_distinct_definitions_and_tests_link_to_them` checks the same for `{example} bad name`.\n- `src/evidence.rs#mod[tests]/fn[invalid_full_names_from_chapter_paths_are_reported_and_not_defined]` checks the validator side:\n  - It reports `invalid requirement name \"bad dir.rules.good\"`.\n  - It expects no evidence file for the invalid name.\n  - It asserts the exact error count.\n\n**An invalid chapter namespace makes the full name invalid**\n- `invalid_names_remain_literal` renders a valid local name in the namespace `a&b` and asserts the output equals the source.\n- `chapters_with_invalid_namespaces_define_nothing_in_the_book` runs `preprocess_book` on `bad dir/rules.md`. It asserts the directive stays literal with no badge, and that a `{impl}` reference to `bad dir.rules.good` stays literal.\n- The `evidence.rs` test above covers the same case for the validator.\n\n**A role with an invalid name stays literal and references nothing**\n- `invalid_names_remain_literal` runs `reference_roles` over the `impl`, `cite`, `test` and `term` roles. It uses invalid names (`bad name`, `a..b`, empty, `a.`) in both markdown and non-markdown modes and expects no matches. A valid name gives exactly one match.\n- `reference_roles` has no branch specific to `fixme`, so the missing `fixme` case in that loop is not a gap. It shares the single name check.\n- `src/fenced.rs#mod[tests]/fn[opening_fences_take_a_role_and_one_valid_or_built_in_name]` covers the fenced form. Names that are invalid (`a b`, `a..b`, `.a`, `a.`, `a/b`, `café.b`) give `None`, and valid names are recognised.\n- `src/lib.rs#mod[tests]/fn[citation_metadata_preserves_prose_other_roles_and_literal_contexts]` also leaves an invalid `{req}` or `{example}` fence name untouched.\n\n**Namespace derivation (omit a leading `reqs/` and a final `index` or `README` segment for nested paths)**\n- `chapter_path_prefixes_the_requirement_name` asserts these results:\n  - `reqs/foo/bar.md` gives `foo.bar`.\n  - `reqs/foo/README.md` and `reqs/foo/index.md` give `foo`.\n  - `foo/index.md` gives `foo`.\n  - `reqs/index.md` gives an empty namespace.\n  - `reqs/bar.md` gives `bar`.\n  - Non-nested `index.md` and `reqs.md` keep their names.\n  - `other/reqs/a.md` keeps `reqs`.\n- `validates_full_names_requirement_text_and_input_lines` shows `reqs/foo/bar.md` yielding `foo.bar.ready` end to end.\n- `invalid_full_names_from_chapter_paths_are_reported_and_not_defined` shows `reqs/index.md` yielding the name `top` end to end.\n\nEvery clause has a test with assertions on the outcome. I found no plausible violation that these tests would accept.",
